The brain-as-antenna/receiver/compiler model is useful if we preserve the epistemic boundaries:

* Antenna: bodies broadcast heat, odor, sound, vibration, posture, movement, gaze, timing, and autonomic changes.
* Receiver: sensory systems collect far more information than focal attention reports.
* Compiler: the nervous system fuses incomplete cues and produces “someone is behind me” or “something is wrong.”
* Projector: prediction fills gaps and proposes the hidden cause.

So many “extra senses” may not require unknown receptors. They can be higher-resolution compilation of weak ordinary signals. Experiments have found very rapid processing of peripheral threat cues, even without reported awareness, while sensorimotor conflicts can experimentally produce the vivid feeling that somebody is standing behind you. Peripheral threat study⁠￼ Felt-presence experiment⁠￼

